              "text": "{\"problem\": {\"theorem\": \"Prove that any two nonabelian groups of order 21 are isomorphic.\", \"assumptions\": [], \"domain\": \"abstract_algebra\", \"explicit_subgoals\": []}, \"candidate_text\": \"Let \\\\(G\\\\) and \\\\(H\\\\) be nonabelian groups of order \\\\(21\\\\). By Sylow's theorems, the number of Sylow \\\\(7\\\\)-subgroups divides \\\\(3\\\\) and is congruent to \\\\(1\\\\pmod 7\\\\), so each group has a unique, hence normal, Sylow \\\\(7\\\\)-subgroup. Write these as \\\\(B=\\\\langle b\\\\rangle\\\\le G\\\\) and \\\\(D=\\\\langle d\\\\rangle\\\\le H\\\\).\\n\\nBy Cauchy's theorem, choose elements \\\\(a\\\\in G\\\\) and \\\\(c\\\\in H\\\\) of order \\\\(3\\\\). Since \\\\(\\\\langle a\\\\rangle\\\\cap B=\\\\{e\\\\}\\\\) and their orders multiply to \\\\(21\\\\), \\\\(G=B\\\\langle a\\\\rangle\\\\). Similarly, \\\\(H=D\\\\langle c\\\\rangle\\\\).\\n\\nNormality of \\\\(B\\\\) gives \\\\(aba^{-1}=b^i\\\\) for some \\\\(i\\\\in(\\\\mathbb Z/7\\\\mathbb Z)^\\\\times\\\\). Because \\\\(a^3=e\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nb=a^3ba^{-3}=b^{i^3},\\n\\\\]\\nso \\\\(i^3\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod 7\\\\). The solutions are \\\\(i=1,2,4\\\\). If \\\\(i=1\\\\), then \\\\(a\\\\) and \\\\(b\\\\) commute; since they generate \\\\(G\\\\), this would make \\\\(G\\\\) abelian. Thus \\\\(i=2\\\\) or \\\\(4\\\\). Replacing \\\\(a\\\\) by \\\\(a^{-1}\\\\) when necessary changes exponent \\\\(4\\\\) to its inverse \\\\(2\\\\pmod 7\\\\). Hence we may choose \\\\(a\\\\) so that\\n\\\\[\\naba^{-1}=b^2.\\n\\\\]\\nLikewise, we may choose \\\\(c\\\\) so that \\\\(cdc^{-1}=d^2\\\\).\\n\\nEvery element of \\\\(G\\\\) has a unique expression \\\\(b^j a^r\\\\), where \\\\(0\\\\le j<7\\\\) and \\\\(0\\\\le r<3\\\\). The analogous statement holds in \\\\(H\\\\). Define\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\phi(b^j a^r)=d^j c^r.\\n\\\\]\\nThis is therefore a well-defined bijection. Moreover,\\n\\\\[\\n(b^j a^r)(b^k a^s)=b^{j+2^r k}a^{r+s},\\n\\\\]\\nwith exponents taken modulo \\\\(7\\\\) and \\\\(3\\\\), respectively. Since the identical multiplication formula holds for \\\\(d^j c^r\\\\), the map \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\) is a homomorphism. Consequently \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\) is an isomorphism, so any two nonabelian groups of order \\\\(21\\\\) are isomorphic.\"}",
