          "text": "{\"issues\":[],\"reason\":\"The proof is correct. Sylow theory yields a unique normal subgroup of order 7; Cauchy's theorem supplies a complement of order 3; nonabelianness forces the conjugation action to be one of the two nontrivial order-3 automorphisms of C7, which become the same action after inverting the order-3 generator. The unique normal forms and matching multiplication laws then establish the claimed isomorphism.\"}",
